# Release signing configuration for rustdar's Android build.
#
# Copy this file to `keystore.properties` (same directory) and fill it in.
# `keystore.properties` and `*.jks` are gitignored: the key and its password
# must never be committed. An earlier keystore password *was* committed, in the
# old cargo-apk `Cargo.toml`, and is in git history -- treat it as burned and do
# not reuse it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this matters
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Without this file `./gradlew assembleRelease` still succeeds, but the artifact
# it produces is `app-<abi>-release-unsigned.apk` -- and an unsigned APK cannot
# be installed. The path of least resistance is then to sideload the *debug*
# APK, which is what the debug signing config makes installable. That build is
# `android:debuggable="true"` and signed with the stock `CN=Android Debug` key,
# so on a real phone it is a debuggable process holding GPS fixes and cached
# configuration, and any app on the device can attach to it. Generating a key
# takes one command; do that instead.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generating a key
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# From this directory (packaging/android). `keytool` ships with the JDK
# that Gradle already requires, so nothing else needs installing:
#
#   keytool -genkeypair -v \
#     -keystore rustdar-release.jks \
#     -alias rustdar \
#     -keyalg RSA -keysize 4096 \
#     -validity 10000 \
#     -storetype PKCS12
#
# It prompts for a password and for the certificate's distinguished name; the DN
# is cosmetic for sideloading, but Google Play shows it. Answer the password
# prompt with something from a password manager and store it there -- the value
# also has to go in `keystore.properties` below, in the clear, which is the
# reason that file is gitignored rather than merely untracked.
#
# `-validity 10000` (about 27 years) is deliberate. Android identifies an app by
# its signing key: once a key expires or is lost, no further update can be
# installed over an existing install, and users have to uninstall first, losing
# their config. Keep the .jks backed up somewhere durable and off this machine.
#
# `-storetype PKCS12` avoids keytool's "proprietary JKS format" migration
# warning on every build. PKCS12 is the JDK default from 9 onwards.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keys
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# storeFile     Path to the keystore. A relative path resolves against
#               packaging/android/ (this directory), not against app/.
# storePassword Password for the keystore itself.
# keyAlias      The -alias given to keytool above.
# keyPassword   Password for that key entry. keytool's PKCS12 flow reuses the
#               store password unless you explicitly set a different one, in
#               which case these two are the same value.

storeFile=rustdar-release.jks
storePassword=CHANGE_ME
keyAlias=rustdar
keyPassword=CHANGE_ME
